      <Heading as="h3" text="useSkinTone" />
      <p>
        Returns the current skin tone as <code>current</code> (which you can also set) and the skin
        tone versions of an emoji as <code>variations</code>. Call it while a component inside
        <code>EmojiPicker.Root</code> starts.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock
        code={`
          <script lang="ts">
            import { useSkinTone } from "@gyen/frimousse";

            const skinTone = useSkinTone("👋");
          </script>

          <!-- (👋) (👋🏻) (👋🏼) (👋🏽) (👋🏾) (👋🏿) -->
          {#each skinTone.variations as variation (variation.skinTone)}
            <button onclick={() => (skinTone.current = variation.skinTone)}>
              {variation.emoji}
            </button>
          {/each}
        `}
      />
      <Heading as="h4" prefix="useSkinTone" text="Parameters" />
      <PropsTable
        rows={[
          {
            name: 'emoji',
            type: 'string | (() => string | undefined)',
            default: '"✋"',
            description:
              'The emoji used to show the skin tones. Pass a function to keep it reactive.',
          },
        ]}
      />

      <Heading as="h3" text="useActiveEmoji" />
      <p>
        Returns the active emoji (hovered, or selected with the keyboard) as <code>current</code>.
        Call it while a component inside <code>EmojiPicker.Root</code> starts.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock
        code={`
          <script lang="ts">
            import { useActiveEmoji } from "@gyen/frimousse";

            const activeEmoji = useActiveEmoji();
          </script>

          {#if activeEmoji.current}
            <span>{activeEmoji.current.emoji} {activeEmoji.current.label}</span>
          {:else}
            <span>Select an emoji…</span>
          {/if}
        `}
      />

      <Heading as="h3" text="defaultEmojiDataResolver" />
      <p>
        Loads and caches Emojibase data, leaving out emojis the browser can't show. It is the
        default resolver of <code>EmojiPicker.Root</code>; call it for locales your own resolver
        doesn't handle.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock
        lang="ts"
        code={`
          import { defaultEmojiDataResolver } from "@gyen/frimousse";

          const data = await defaultEmojiDataResolver("fr", {});
        `}
      />
      <Heading as="h4" prefix="defaultEmojiDataResolver" text="Parameters" />
      <PropsTable
        rows={[
          {
            name: 'locale',
            type: 'Locale',
            required: true,
            description:
              "The locale to load. Falls back to <code>en</code> when Emojibase doesn't support it.",
          },
          {
            name: 'options',
            type: 'object',
            required: true,
            description:
              '<code>emojiVersion</code> and <code>emojibaseUrl</code>, which work like the <a href="#emojipicker.root-props">picker props</a>, and an optional <code>signal</code> to cancel loading. Pass <code>{}</code> for the defaults.',
          },
        ]}
      />
      <p>
        Returns a promise of <code>EmojiData</code>. Loading errors reject the promise.
      </p>

      <Heading as="h3" text="createEmojiDataCache" />
      <p>
        Creates a cache that keeps custom emoji data in <code>localStorage</code>. Read and write it
        in your resolver to reuse data across visits. Give it your own name to keep it apart from
        the default resolver's data.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock
        lang="ts"
        code={`
          import {
            createEmojiDataCache,
            defaultEmojiDataResolver,
            type EmojiData,
            type EmojiDataResolver,
          } from "@gyen/frimousse";

          const cache = createEmojiDataCache({ name: "my-app/emoji-data" });

          export const resolveEmojiData: EmojiDataResolver = async (locale, options) => {
            if (locale !== "ne") return defaultEmojiDataResolver(locale, options);

            const cached = cache.get(locale);

            if (cached) return cached.data;

            const response = await fetch("/emoji-data/ne.json", { signal: options.signal });

            if (!response.ok) throw new Error("Could not load emoji data");

            const data: EmojiData = await response.json();
            cache.set(locale, data);

            return data;
          };
        `}
      />
      <p>
        Entries stay until you replace or remove them; the cache doesn't check for updates. If
        browser storage isn't available, reads return <code>null</code> and writes do nothing.
      </p>
      <Heading as="h4" prefix="createEmojiDataCache" text="Parameters" />
      <PropsTable
        rows={[
          {
            name: 'options.name',
            type: 'string',
            default: '"frimousse/data"',
            description: 'The cache name. Each entry is stored under its locale.',
          },
        ]}
      />
      <Heading as="h4" prefix="createEmojiDataCache" text="Methods" />
      <PropsTable
        rows={[
          {
            name: 'get(locale)',
            description:
              'Returns <code>{ data, metadata }</code>, or <code>null</code> if the entry is missing or broken.',
          },
          {
            name: 'set(locale, data, metadata?)',
            description: 'Stores data and optional metadata for a locale, replacing any old entry.',
          },
          { name: 'delete(locale)', description: 'Removes the entry for a locale.' },
          { name: 'clear()', description: 'Removes all entries with this cache name.' },
        ]}
      />

      <Heading as="h3" text="getEmojiDetails" />
      <p>
        Reads an emoji's localized details from data already in memory, loaded by a picker or
        <code>loadEmojiDetails</code>. It never fetches, reads browser storage, or calls a resolver.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock
        lang="ts"
        code={`
          import { getEmojiDetails } from "@gyen/frimousse";

          const details = getEmojiDetails("👍🏽", { locale: "en" });

          details?.label; // "Thumbs up"
          details?.emoji; // "👍"
        `}
      />
      <Heading as="h4" prefix="getEmojiDetails" text="Parameters" />
      <PropsTable
        rows={[
          {
            name: 'emoji',
            type: 'string',
            required: true,
            description:
              "The emoji to look up. Skin tone versions return the base emoji's details.",
          },
          {
            name: 'options.locale',
            type: 'Locale',
            default: '"en"',
            description: 'The locale to use.',
          },
          {
            name: 'options.emojibaseUrl',
            type: 'string',
            description: 'The Emojibase source, like the picker prop.',
          },
          {
            name: 'options.emojiVersion',
            type: 'number',
            description:
              "Picks the same versioned source as the picker prop. Lookups don't remove emojis the browser can't show.",
          },
          {
            name: 'options.resolveEmojiData',
            type: 'EmojiDataResolver',
            description:
              'Points to a custom data source without calling it. Pass the same function and options the picker used.',
          },
        ]}
      />
      <Heading as="h4" prefix="getEmojiDetails" text="Return value" />
      <p>
        <code>EmojiDetails</code>, or <code>undefined</code> if the data isn't loaded or nothing
        matches. Its fields:
      </p>
      <PropsTable
        rows={[
          { name: 'emoji', type: 'string', description: 'The base emoji, without a skin tone.' },
          { name: 'label', type: 'string', description: 'The localized name.' },
          { name: 'tags', type: 'string[]', description: 'The localized search tags.' },
          {
            name: 'category',
            type: 'number',
            description: "Matches a category's <code>index</code>.",
          },
          {
            name: 'version',
            type: 'number',
            description: 'The Emoji version that added this emoji.',
          },
          {
            name: 'countryFlag',
            type: 'true | undefined',
            description: 'Whether the emoji is a country flag.',
          },
          {
            name: 'skins',
            type: 'object | undefined',
            description: 'Skin tone versions, by skin tone.',
          },
          {
            name: 'aliases',
            type: 'string[] | undefined',
            description: 'Other sequences that match, like mixed skin tones.',
          },
        ]}
      />

      <Heading as="h3" text="loadEmojiDetails" />
      <p>
        Like <code>getEmojiDetails</code>, but returns a promise and loads the data first if needed.
        It works outside <code>EmojiPicker.Root</code> and shares loaded data and pending requests
        with pickers. This replaces React's <code>useEmojiDetails</code>: use it with{' '}
        <code>{'{#await}'}</code>.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock
        lang="ts"
        code={`
          import { loadEmojiDetails } from "@gyen/frimousse";

          const details = await loadEmojiDetails("👍🏽", { locale: "en" });
        `}
      />
      <p>
        It takes the same emoji and options as{' '}
        <a href="#getemojidetails">
          <code>getEmojiDetails</code>
        </a>
        . A custom <code>resolveEmojiData</code> is called when its data isn't loaded yet, with the
        locale,
        <code>emojiVersion</code> and <code>emojibaseUrl</code> (no <code>signal</code>); calls with
        the same function and options share one request. Loading errors reject the promise and are
        kept for that source, until a picker loads the same source successfully.
      </p>

      <Heading as="h2" text="Differences from React" />
      <p>The API follows the React version. Where Svelte has its own way, it uses it:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Render props are snippets: <code>EmojiPicker.List</code>'s <code>components</code> prop
          becomes the
          <code>categoryHeader</code>, <code>row</code> and <code>emoji</code> snippets, and
          <code>Empty</code>, <code>ActiveEmoji</code> and <code>SkinTone</code> take a
          <code>children</code> snippet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>EmojiPicker.Search</code> uses <code>bind:value</code> instead of <code>value</code>{' '}
          and
          <code>onChange</code>, and <code>EmojiPicker.Root</code> supports{' '}
          <code>bind:skinTone</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Every element part has a bindable <code>ref</code> instead of React refs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>useSkinTone</code> and <code>useActiveEmoji</code> return objects with a reactive
          <code>current</code> instead of tuples.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>useEmojiDetails</code> (which needs Suspense) is <code>loadEmojiDetails</code>,
          which returns a promise.
        </li>
      </ul>

